When a Pope offered to free Saint Clare from her vow, she told him she never wanted to be released from following Christ. As a girl of a knightly house in Assisi, she had slipped away at night through a door barred with logs and a stone column. She then spent forty-two years in the small convent of San Damiano, nearly thirty of them ill. How did she hold on to owning nothing against her family and a Pope's kind offer, and keep her sisters safe from the emperor's soldiers, until she kissed the Pope's seal on her poverty as she lay dying?
